-- allium: 3

-- Module: reconcile.
-- Scope: the per-pod loop. A poll's reading becomes a pod state (the §5 state
-- diagram): the window during which an applied value stands after readings
-- stop being valid, the neutral write that ends it, the contract-absent state
-- with its Event and gauge, the unknown gauge, and a pod without a pod IP
-- (§5, §8 failure modes, §9.6). The decided loop, desired state against last
-- applied (ownership Applied), a patch only on meaningful change and only
-- where may_write allows, a throttled change pending, no patch once
-- deletionTimestamp is set (§4) and no special handling of a rollout (§9.13),
-- is the Loop contract, which ticket 31 stated with tend on 2026-10-13 and
-- built. What a reading is is
-- worker_contract's (§9.4a); the mapped value is cost_mapping's (§9.5); the
-- patch is ownership's (§9.10); whether a due write may go now is budget's
-- (§9.8); the Event, gauge and line names are observability's (§9.14); the
-- marker's write times are informational, and cleanup is lifecycle's
-- (§9.12, ticket 23).
-- Owner: ticket 21 settled every §9.6 question here with the maintainer on
-- 2026-10-13. Ticket 31 built it with the decided loop (Loop), and runs it
-- in a thin driver that 46 replaces with budget's schedule and bucket (Cycle).
-- Amended by ticket 23 on 2026-10-13: Neutral and Restart name lifecycle's
-- answer, no expiry and the record a neutral removal leaves falling to
-- cleanup.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One pod's place in the reading state machine, kept in memory by the
-- reconcile process for this run. kind is fresh before any reading in this
-- process and after a valid one; stale while readings are not valid and the
-- window has not elapsed; unknown once it has; absent once a run of not_found
-- or refused readings has lasted absent_after. "Known" in prose means fresh
-- or stale, the initial state included: the pod's applied value is treated
-- as current. failed is the
-- number of consecutive readings since the last valid one that were not
-- valid. run_since is the time of the first reading of the current unbroken
-- run of not_found or refused readings, null when there is no run.
value PodState {
    kind: fresh | stale | unknown | absent
    failed: Integer
    run_since: Timestamp?
}

-- One poll's reading as the loop consumes it: worker_contract's kind, and,
-- for a valid reading, the cost and whether the worker is draining (accepting
-- false); cost is null and draining false for every other kind.
value Reading {
    kind: ReadingKind
    cost: Integer?
    draining: Boolean
}

-- The parse of the marker's applied record for pod-deletion-cost (ownership
-- Applied) to cost_mapping's value: usable with value 0 for a null or missing
-- record, usable with the value for a canonical int32 string of -1 or 1 to
-- 2147483647 (cost_mapping Canonical), and not usable, with value 0, for any
-- other string, "0", "+5" and "007" among them, which stands as a conflict
-- (Conflict).
value Applied {
    value: Integer
    usable: Boolean
}

-- The desired state of the two owned annotations on one pod: the
-- pod-deletion-cost value as cost_mapping's Mapped, 0 for the removal, and
-- the safe-to-evict string, null for absent or the removal.
value Desired {
    cost: Integer
    safe_to_evict: String?
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- worker_contract's Reading kinds, verbatim.
enum ReadingKind { valid | not_found | invalid | refused | timed_out | unreachable }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract Fallback {
    initial: () -> PodState
    observe: (state: PodState, reading: ReadingKind, at: Timestamp, unknown_polls: Integer, absent_after: Duration) -> PodState
    neutral: (state: PodState) -> Boolean

    @invariant Readings
        -- initial is fresh with failed 0 and no run, and only a reading
        -- moves the state: observe is called once per reading a poll
        -- produces, with at the time it was produced, and nothing else
        -- calls it. A poll budget's NoOverlap skips and a response budget's
        -- Leaving discards produce no reading and leave the state as it
        -- is, so under load the windows
        -- counted in readings stretch with the effective interval (budget
        -- Concurrency). When the clauses below would disagree about kind, a
        -- valid reading wins and makes the pod fresh; otherwise a pod whose
        -- run has lasted absent_after is absent (Absent); otherwise failed
        -- decides between stale and unknown (Window).
    @invariant Window
        -- A valid reading makes the pod fresh, with failed 0 and run_since
        -- null, whatever its state. Any other reading, timed_out,
        -- unreachable, invalid, not_found and refused alike, adds one to
        -- failed; the pod is stale while failed is below unknown_polls and
        -- unknown once it reaches it, unless Absent says absent, in which
        -- case failed keeps counting and decides nothing. unknown_polls is
        -- config.unknown_polls, 3 by default, valid from 1 to 100,
        -- install-wide with no per-Deployment override (config Keys and
        -- Installed); at 1 the first reading that is not valid makes the pod
        -- unknown. At the defaults the window is about 90 to 100 seconds
        -- after the last valid reading, three readings at a 30-second
        -- interval with jitter; at a 10-second override about 30 seconds and
        -- at a 300-second override 15 to 17 minutes, since the window is
        -- counted in readings and not in time (ticket 24). A stale pod keeps
        -- its applied value and is not rewritten: missing data is never busy
        -- and never drained (§5).
    @invariant Neutral
        -- neutral is true for exactly the kinds unknown and absent. While a
        -- pod is neutral its desired state for every owned annotation is
        -- the removal: every owned annotation standing owned under
        -- ownership's Adoption, pod-deletion-cost (a drained -1 included)
        -- and safe-to-evict alike, is removed in one patch with purpose
        -- apply (observability Purpose); a safe-to-evict record the
        -- Deployment no longer enables is among them, so a value an opt-out
        -- left is removed here when the pod goes neutral before lifecycle's
        -- cleanup reaches it, and the null record this leaves still falls
        -- to that cleanup, which drops it from the marker (lifecycle
        -- Cleanup Scope and Marker). The patch is due at once on entry and
        -- sent as budget's PerPod, Bucket and Pending allow. The marker
        -- stays, with a null applied record for each removed annotation
        -- (ownership
        -- Applied), and the pod stays managed: a rejected neutral write is
        -- a rejection with purpose apply (ownership Rejected, observability
        -- Events Rejected), and purpose cleanup is lifecycle's alone, for a
        -- pod or an annotation that leaves knarr's management (lifecycle
        -- Cleanup Scope, ticket 23).
        -- The removal is dropped for every owned annotation whose record
        -- is null or missing (ownership WriteMode), and no patch is sent to
        -- a terminating pod (k8s_client NoPatchWhileTerminating), so a pod
        -- that never had a valid reading has nothing to remove and stays
        -- unannotated until its first valid one (§5). The next valid
        -- reading maps against the absent applied value (cost_mapping Absent
        -- and Crossing), so a busy pod is rewritten at once, as budget
        -- allows. For safe-to-evict this removal is the one rule here;
        -- safe_to_evict (ticket 25) may
        -- replace that one sentence if it settles another value for a
        -- protection that ends for lack of data.
    @invariant Absent
        -- A not_found or refused reading starts a run, setting run_since to
        -- at when it is null, or continues one; before entry any other
        -- kind, valid, invalid, timed_out or unreachable, ends the run and
        -- sets run_since to null, so invalid never counts toward a run and
        -- a 5xx storm is transient under Window. The pod enters absent at a
        -- not_found or refused reading whose at is at least absent_after
        -- after run_since, so a run needs two readings at least;
        -- absent_after is config.absent_after, 300 seconds by default, valid
        -- from 30 to 3600 seconds, install-wide with no per-Deployment
        -- override (config Keys and Installed). At the defaults that is
        -- about eleven readings at a 30-second interval, two at a 300-second
        -- override and thirty-one at a 10-second one: a slow starter has five
        -- minutes to bind its endpoint, and a Deployment whose pods never
        -- serve the contract is reported within about five minutes plus one
        -- interval whatever its interval. Once absent the pod leaves on a
        -- valid reading only, which makes it fresh under Window; a
        -- not_found, refused, timed_out, unreachable or invalid reading
        -- after entry leaves kind absent and run_since as it is and raises
        -- nothing. After leaving, the next not_found or refused reading
        -- starts a new run, and entry at the end of it is a re-entry
        -- (observability Cadence OnEntry). A NetworkPolicy block reads as
        -- timed_out (worker_contract NetworkPolicy), so it never makes a
        -- contract absent: the pod goes neutral under Window and shows on
        -- knarr_polls_total with reading timed_out. On entry any owned
        -- annotation still standing is removed under Neutral, knarr makes
        -- the one create attempt of the ContractAbsent Event observability's
        -- Events ContractAbsent names, on the pod's Deployment by name and
        -- uid, with the Deployment's effective status port and path (config
        -- Effective) and the reading that entered it, and logs the warning
        -- line (observability Logs StateLines); the pod counts on
        -- knarr_pods_contract_absent while it stays (Gauges); and polling
        -- continues on its normal schedule (budget Jitter), so a pod that
        -- binds late is managed from its first valid reading. The state
        -- machine runs for every pod that is polled, a terminating one
        -- included; the caller leaves a terminating pod, and one the LIST no
        -- longer shows, out of the current entries it hands observability's
        -- entered, so it leaves every announced state and the Event, the
        -- line and the gauges exclude it (observability Cadence OnEntry,
        -- Catalogue SeriesLife).
    @invariant Gauges
        -- knarr_pods_unknown{deployment} is the number of the Deployment's
        -- pods whose kind is unknown and knarr_pods_contract_absent
        -- {deployment} the number whose kind is absent, as observability's
        -- Standing names them: the two are disjoint, a stale pod counts on
        -- neither, a terminating pod counts on neither (observability
        -- SeriesLife), and both are rebuilt from this process's states after
        -- a restart. Entering unknown raises no Event and logs no state
        -- line; only absent is announced on entry.
    @invariant NoPodIp
        -- A pod without a pod IP (k8s_client Pod.pod_ip null) is a target:
        -- it is in config's target set, counts in knarr_targets and is among
        -- the listed pods budget's on_list receives. The caller builds no
        -- joining entry for it (budget Leaving), so it is not scheduled, not
        -- polled and has no state until a discovery cycle shows an IP, when
        -- the caller builds its joining entry and it joins with a fresh
        -- phase (budget Phase) and the initial state. A just-started pod has
        -- no window of its own: nothing was ever written on it, so it stays
        -- unannotated until its first valid reading (§5; the loop's row in
        -- ticket 31).
    @invariant Restart
        -- The state is in memory only. After a restart every pod is initial,
        -- so a pod holding a value keeps it until the window elapses again
        -- in the new process: nothing is removed at startup for lack of a
        -- reading and nothing flaps (§3). An absent pod leaves
        -- knarr_pods_contract_absent and is polled afresh; it re-enters, and
        -- raises ContractAbsent once more, once a new run has lasted
        -- absent_after, and knarr_pods_unknown and knarr_pods_contract_absent
        -- are 0 for every Deployment until a window elapses, from the first
        -- discovery cycle at which observability's SeriesLife rebuilds the
        -- pod-state gauges. This follows budget's Restart,
        -- config's InvalidValue memory and observability's Cadence Restart.
        -- The marker's at and each record's at are not read here, and
        -- decide nothing anywhere: they are informational, and a value a
        -- restarted knarr
        -- finds on a marked pod never expires by age; this window, elapsing
        -- again in the new process, is its only expiry (lifecycle Restart
        -- NoExpiry and Informational, ticket 23).
    @invariant NotReadyWithCost
        -- worker_contract's ReadinessWarning counts a pod only while its
        -- reading is known: a pod is in observability's not_ready_with_cost
        -- state while its Ready condition is false, its kind is fresh or
        -- stale, and the pod-deletion-cost value its marker records as
        -- applied (ownership Applied), which is what its last valid reading
        -- mapped to under cost_mapping, is above 0; so after a restart a
        -- NotReady pod carrying such a value is in the state from the first
        -- discovery cycle, in the initial state, while its reading stays
        -- known (Restart). A pod whose kind is unknown or absent has no current
        -- valid reading and leaves the state; it re-enters, with its line,
        -- when a valid reading maps above 0 again while it is NotReady
        -- (observability Cadence OnEntry). The gauge is
        -- knarr_pods_not_ready_with_cost (observability Standing).
    @invariant Pure
        -- initial, observe and neutral are functions of their arguments
        -- alone: the clock and both windows are passed in, and none reads
        -- state, time, config or the pod, or sends. Keeping each pod's state,
        -- sending the neutral write, creating the Event, logging the line
        -- and serving the gauges are the reconcile process's (ticket 31),
        -- under supervision.
}

contract Loop {
    parse_applied: (record: String?) -> Applied
    safe_to_evict_usable: (record: String?) -> Boolean
    desired: (state: PodState, reading: Reading, applied: Applied, safe_to_evict: String?, thresholds: List<Integer>, margin: Integer) -> Desired
    due: (desired: Desired, applied: Applied, safe_to_evict: String?) -> List<String>

    @invariant Desired
        -- The desired state of a pod is the mapped cost value, the
        -- safe-to-evict value and the marker (§4.3). desired reads the
        -- pod's state and the reading the cycle's poll produced, after
        -- observe has taken that reading: while the pod is neutral
        -- (Neutral) the desired cost is 0 and the desired safe-to-evict is
        -- null, the removal of both; otherwise a valid reading maps through
        -- cost_mapping's map with the reading's clamped cost, its draining,
        -- applied.value as the last applied value and the Deployment's
        -- effective thresholds and margin (config Effective), and any other
        -- reading leaves the desired cost at applied.value, so a stale pod
        -- keeps its value (Window). The desired safe-to-evict outside the
        -- removal is the applied safe-to-evict record as it stands: knarr
        -- writes no safe-to-evict value until safe_to_evict (ticket 25)
        -- settles what it writes, so an opt-in enables standing and the
        -- gauge (observability Standing) and nothing else yet. applied is
        -- parse_applied of the applied record's value for pod-deletion-cost,
        -- which is the value cost_mapping's Pure names: the marker's, never
        -- the annotation's own text. A reading maps against the applied
        -- value the cycle's view holds, so after a restart the first valid
        -- reading maps against what the marker records and nothing flaps
        -- (Restart).
    @invariant Due
        -- A patch is made only when the desired state differs from the last
        -- applied state (§4.4): due returns pod-deletion-cost when
        -- desired.cost differs from applied.value and safe-to-evict when
        -- desired.safe_to_evict differs from the applied safe-to-evict
        -- record, in that order, and nothing else. That one comparison
        -- covers a threshold crossing (cost_mapping Crossing), the first
        -- annotation of a pod, whose applied value is 0 with no record, the
        -- startup repair of a pod whose marker records a value its reading
        -- no longer maps to, the removal that spells 0 or the neutral
        -- fallback (Neutral), and a safe-to-evict flip. The loop asks
        -- ownership's standing and may_write for each owned annotation it
        -- considers, pod-deletion-cost always and safe-to-evict while the
        -- marker holds a record for it or the Deployment enables it
        -- (ownership Adoption), and hands ownership's patch only the due
        -- writes may_write allows: a write of null for an annotation whose
        -- record is null or missing is dropped, and a patch left with no
        -- writes sends nothing (ownership WriteMode). The write carries
        -- cost_mapping's render of the desired cost, so the emitted string
        -- is canonical (§6).
    @invariant Conflict
        -- A record that does not parse as its annotation's value stands as
        -- a conflict (ownership Adoption and Applied): for pod-deletion-cost
        -- an applied record parse_applied finds not usable, and for
        -- safe-to-evict an applied record safe_to_evict_usable rejects,
        -- which is any string but "true" and "false" (null is usable). The
        -- loop treats such a standing as conflict whatever ownership's
        -- standing says of the annotation's text, so the annotation is
        -- yielded with ownership's yield and never fed to map, and a write
        -- of it that due names is dropped by ownership's may_write, so
        -- nothing of it is written.
    @invariant Pending
        -- A change that is due but may not go now stays pending and is not
        -- dropped (§4.4; budget Pending). Nothing is stored for it: when the
        -- pod's turn comes the change is re-derived with desired from the
        -- pod's latest reading against the applied value the newest view
        -- holds, so a change that is no longer due has nothing pending.
        -- Under Cycle every due write goes at once, so nothing is pending
        -- between cycles until 46 builds the bucket.
    @invariant Sequence
        -- The view a patch is built from is the newest API-returned one:
        -- the cycle's LIST or knarr's own last accepted patch response,
        -- later by the order received (ownership Adoption). A write and a
        -- yield on one pod in one cycle go in sequence, the yield first and
        -- the write built from the yield's response, and an accepted patch
        -- response replaces the pod's view for the rest of the cycle. The
        -- marker a yield is reported from, and the Event, are ownership's
        -- Event: the create is attempted only after the yield patch is
        -- accepted.
    @invariant Rejected422
        -- A 422 is handled from the next LIST, with no GET and no retry in
        -- that cycle (ownership Precondition): the loop keeps the failed
        -- patch's operations for the pod until the next LIST and no longer,
        -- classifies them against that LIST's view of the pod with
        -- ownership's rejected, and forgets them when the LIST arrives,
        -- whatever rejected says. The handling of a rejection is ticket
        -- 32's (ownership Rejected, observability Events Rejected). The
        -- kept operations are in memory only: a restart forgets them and
        -- the write is simply rebuilt.
    @invariant Terminating
        -- No patch is made once a pod's deletionTimestamp is set in the
        -- newest view, and no yield either; polling it continues while the
        -- LIST shows it, and observe keeps its state (§4.5, Absent;
        -- k8s_client NoPatchWhileTerminating). A patch in flight when the
        -- deletion lands fails its deletionTimestamp test with 422 and is
        -- forgotten under Rejected422.
    @invariant JustStarted
        -- A pod that has just started stays unannotated until its first
        -- valid poll (§5): before any reading in this process its state is
        -- initial, its reading is none, so desired is its applied value and
        -- nothing is due; a pod without a pod IP has no reading, no state
        -- and no write until a LIST shows one (NoPodIp). No annotation is
        -- written before scale-down and none ahead of a reading: costs stay
        -- fresh all the time, cycle by cycle (§4, §7).
    @invariant Rollout
        -- A Deployment rollout gets no special handling (§9.13): the pods
        -- of the old and the new ReplicaSet are both targets (config
        -- Discovery), each is polled and mapped on its own, and a pod the
        -- rollout terminates is a terminating pod (Terminating).
    @invariant Cycle
        -- Until the poll schedule and the patcher are built (budget
        -- PollSchedule and WriteBudget, ticket 46), the reconcile process is
        -- a thin driver on the LIST timer: on its own start and then every
        -- config.list_interval it runs one discovery cycle (config
        -- Discovery), polls every target pod with a pod IP once, at most
        -- config.poll_concurrency polls at a time, observes each reading,
        -- and sends each due write and yield at once through k8s_client's
        -- patch_pod, with no per-pod patch interval, no bucket and no pause;
        -- a 429 is a failed patch left to the next cycle. The cycle reads
        -- the token and the namespace first (k8s_client TokenReload); a
        -- failed read, or a LIST that fails, ends the cycle with an error
        -- line, the previous target set stands, and the next tick tries
        -- again. The cycle latches readiness once the first cycle's three
        -- LISTs have succeeded, before that cycle's polls and writes:
        -- readiness is the discovery cycle's (observability Probes
        -- Readiness), not the polls'. It rebuilds the per-Deployment gauges and retires a
        -- Deployment that left (observability SeriesLife), at once, since
        -- nothing is pending or in flight between its cycles. Ticket 46
        -- replaces the poll fan-out and the direct patch with the schedule
        -- and the bucket, and nothing here depends on their absence. The
        -- next tick is armed config.list_interval after a cycle ends, so
        -- cycles never overlap. The cycle reads the clock once, after its
        -- LISTs, and that reading is the time of every reading and write of
        -- the cycle, the at ownership's Recorded marker holds among them.
        -- The polls run in workers linked to the reconcile process and
        -- bounded by its wait, not under a supervisor of their own: a
        -- worker that crashes crashes the process, which its supervisor
        -- restarts, and a worker that has not answered when the wait ends
        -- gives its pod a timed_out reading (worker_contract Transport) and
        -- no other pod's. That is the one exception to a supervisor per
        -- process in knarr, and it ends with the fan-out when 46 builds
        -- the schedule.
    @invariant Pure
        -- parse_applied, safe_to_evict_usable, desired and due are
        -- functions of their arguments alone: the state, the reading, the
        -- view's applied records and the Deployment's settings are passed
        -- in, and none reads state, time, config or the pod, or sends. The
        -- cycle, the per-pod memory and every send are the reconcile
        -- process's, under supervision.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    unknown_polls: Integer = 3
    absent_after: Duration = 300.seconds
}
